{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>That has four legs.</b> In Torat Kohanim they expounded that this comes to permit one that has five. And it appears that the same ruling applies to one that has six and seven, and not specifically five. And one needs to understand for what purpose it permitted a five-legged [creature] by inference, and did not state explicitly: ״every flying creeping thing that has five legs״ or in this manner: ״from four legs and upward you may eat.״ And perhaps it was necessary to teach us as well the ruling permitting the extract of unclean locusts, which they, of blessed memory (in Torat Kohanim), derived from the verse's saying in this very passage ״sheketz hu״ — excluding its admixtures, which is the extract (ציר), as they stated there. And therefore it chose to teach us the permissibility of a five-legged [creature] in this manner [i.e., by inference]."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:20.383946730Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}